---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro'
import PageLinks from '../../components/PageLinks.astro'
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'create-tables', text: 'Create tables' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'alter-tables', text: 'Alter tables' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'write-data', text: 'Write data' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'update-and-delete', text: 'Update and delete' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'copy', text: 'COPY streaming' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'views-and-temporary-tables', text: 'Views and temporary tables' },
]
---
<DocsLayout title="Tables and data" description="Create and alter thinDB tables and write, update, delete, or stream rows." headings={headings}>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="SQL guide" title="Tables and data" description="Define strict columnar tables, load rows directly or from queries, and use update, delete, upsert, views, materialized views, and streaming COPY where the workload needs them." />
  <h2 id="create-tables">Create tables</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`CREATE TABLE invoices (
  invoice_id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  customer_id BIGINT NOT NULL,
  issued_on DATE NOT NULL,
  amount DECIMAL(18, 2) NOT NULL,
  memo VARCHAR(256) DEFAULT NULL,
  created_at DATETIME DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  updated_at DATETIME DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

CREATE TABLE events (
  account_id BIGINT,
  occurred_at DATETIME,
  payload JSON
) ORDER BY (account_id, occurred_at);

CREATE TABLE invoice_summary AS
SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total
FROM invoices
GROUP BY customer_id;`} />
  <p>Every table is sorted on an order key. <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> makes it unique, with last-writer-wins resolution of repeated keys. <code>ORDER BY (cols)</code> (or StarRocks' <code>DUPLICATE KEY (cols)</code>) gives a non-unique key that keeps every row. With no key clause the table orders by its first column. Choose the key your queries filter and group on: segments and row groups are pruned by it.</p>
  <p>SQL columns are nullable by default; <code>PRIMARY KEY</code>, <code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>SERIAL</code>, and <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> make the relevant column non-nullable. Defaults may be a literal, <code>NULL</code>, or <code>CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code>; a <code>DATETIME</code> column may also declare <code>ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code>.</p>
  <p>Supported lifecycle statements include <code>CREATE TABLE [IF NOT EXISTS]</code>, <code>CREATE TABLE AS SELECT</code>, <code>ALTER TABLE</code>, <code>RENAME TABLE</code>, <code>TRUNCATE TABLE</code>, and <code>DROP TABLE [IF EXISTS]</code>. A <code>CREATE TABLE AS SELECT</code> whose query fails leaves no table behind.</p>

  <h2 id="alter-tables">Alter tables</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`ALTER TABLE invoices ADD COLUMN currency VARCHAR(3) DEFAULT 'USD' AFTER amount;
ALTER TABLE invoices ADD (region VARCHAR(16), channel VARCHAR(16));
ALTER TABLE invoices RENAME COLUMN memo TO note;
ALTER TABLE invoices DROP COLUMN channel;
ALTER TABLE invoices AUTO_INCREMENT = 100000;`} />
  <p><code>ADD [COLUMN]</code> takes <code>FIRST</code> or <code>AFTER col</code>, or a parenthesized list added at the end. <code>DROP COLUMN</code>, <code>RENAME COLUMN</code>, <code>RENAME TO</code>, and <code>AUTO_INCREMENT = n</code> are supported. A column of a non-unique order key can be dropped; a <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> column cannot (<code>UniqueKeyColumnDrop</code>). Changing an existing column's type is not supported yet.</p>
  <p>ALTER rewrites the table into a shadow copy and swaps it in atomically. Reads see the old version until the swap; writes wait for it. Deletes and key filters carry over.</p>

  <h2 id="write-data">Write data</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`INSERT INTO invoices (customer_id, issued_on, amount)
VALUES
  (42, DATE '2026-07-01', 125.00),
  (42, DATE '2026-07-08', 75.50);

INSERT INTO invoice_archive
SELECT * FROM invoices WHERE issued_on < DATE '2026-01-01';

INSERT INTO invoices (invoice_id, customer_id, issued_on, amount)
VALUES (1002, 42, DATE '2026-07-08', 80.00)
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE amount = VALUES(amount);

INSERT INTO invoices SET customer_id = 7, issued_on = CURRENT_DATE, amount = 10.00;`} />
  <ul>
    <li>On a unique table, an <code>INSERT</code> whose column list leaves some columns out is a partial update when the key already exists: the listed columns change and the others keep their values.</li>
    <li><code>REPLACE INTO</code> replaces the whole row, so columns it leaves out take their defaults.</li>
    <li><code>INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE</code>, <code>INSERT ... SET</code>, and insert-from-select are supported. <code>DEFAULT</code> works as a value in <code>VALUES</code>, <code>SET</code>, and <code>UPDATE</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="update-and-delete">Update and delete</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`UPDATE invoices
SET amount = amount * 1.02,
    note = 'adjusted'
WHERE issued_on < DATE '2026-07-01';

UPDATE invoices i JOIN customers c ON c.customer_id = i.customer_id
SET i.region = c.region;

DELETE FROM invoices
WHERE customer_id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM churned);`} />
  <p>Update expressions may reference existing columns, scalar functions, session variables, scalar subqueries, and joined tables. Deletes create tombstones for persisted segments and remove matching memtable rows; compaction later folds tombstones away. Each <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> commits once, and readers never see part of it. A statement that stages more than 64 MB of change commits in 64 MB chunks.</p>

  <h2 id="copy">COPY streaming</h2>
  <p>The PostgreSQL wire supports <code>COPY table [(columns)] FROM STDIN</code> and <code>COPY ... TO STDOUT</code> in text format.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`COPY invoices (invoice_id, customer_id, issued_on, amount)
FROM STDIN WITH (FORMAT TEXT);

COPY invoices TO STDOUT WITH (FORMAT TEXT);`} />
  <Callout type="note" title="COPY scope"><p><code>FORMAT CSV</code>, <code>COPY FROM 'path'</code>, and <code>COPY TO 'path'</code> are rejected. File reads use guarded table sources such as <code>read_csv</code> and require <code>--file-root</code>.</p></Callout>

  <h2 id="views-and-temporary-tables">Views and temporary tables</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`CREATE VIEW open_invoices AS
SELECT * FROM invoices WHERE amount > 0;

CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW customer_totals AS
SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM invoices GROUP BY customer_id;

REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW customer_totals;

CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE current_batch (invoice_id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL(18, 2));

DROP VIEW open_invoices;`} />
  <p>A plain view stores its query and expands inline wherever it is read. A materialized view stores its result as a table. <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</code> recomputes it and swaps the new result in only once the query succeeds; nothing refreshes it automatically. Temporary tables belong to the session namespace and disappear with the session.</p>
  <PageLinks previous={{ label: 'Connect a client', href: '/connect/' }} next={{ label: 'Query data', href: '/sql/queries/' }} />
</DocsLayout>
